      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is an AQL calculator and how is it used in garment manufacturing?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "An AQL (Acceptable Quality Level) calculator determines how many garments to inspect from a production lot and how many defects are allowable before rejecting the shipment. Based on ISO 2859-1 (ANSI/ASQ Z1.4), it takes your lot size and inspection level and outputs a sample size and accept/reject numbers. AQL 2.5 is the garment industry standard — it means a maximum 2.5% defective rate is acceptable. SDF Clothing applies AQL 2.5 on every order before packing."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is GSM in clothing and how do I convert it to ounces?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "GSM stands for Grams per Square Metre — the standard measure of fabric weight. Higher GSM means heavier, denser fabric. To convert GSM to oz/yd², divide by 33.906. To convert oz/yd² to GSM, multiply by 33.906. A standard T-shirt fabric is 170–200 GSM (5–6 oz). A standard hoodie fleece is 300–340 GSM (8.9–10 oz). Specifying GSM correctly in your tech pack is critical for accurate factory pricing and correct hand-feel."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is fabric shrinkage and how is it calculated?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Fabric shrinkage is the reduction in a fabric's dimensions after washing or processing. It is calculated as: Shrinkage % = [(Initial Measurement – Final Measurement) / Initial Measurement] × 100. For garment production, shrinkage must be measured in both warp (length) and weft (width) directions. Cotton typically shrinks 2–5%. A shrinkage rate below 3% is generally acceptable for most retail buyers. Manufacturers apply pre-shrinking treatments to control shrinkage before cutting."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What HS code do I use for clothing imports?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Clothing HS codes fall under Chapters 61 (knitted or crocheted garments) and 62 (woven garments). T-shirts are HS 6109, hoodies and sweatshirts are HS 6110, woven trousers are HS 6203/6204, and jackets are HS 6201/6202. The correct HS code determines your import duty rate. Misclassification can result in penalties and retrospective duty assessments. For Bangladesh exports to the EU and UK, the correct HS code is also required to claim duty-free EBA/DCTS access."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What are GINETEX care label symbols?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "GINETEX care symbols are the international standard for textile care labelling under ISO 3758. They use five base shapes: washtub (washing), triangle (bleaching), square (drying), iron (ironing), and circle (professional care). Numbers inside the washtub indicate maximum wash temperature in °C. An X through any symbol means 'do not'. EU textile labelling regulations require care symbols on all garments sold in Europe. The sequence must always follow: washing → bleaching → drying → ironing → professional care."
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
