    <h2>Free Garment Manufacturing Tools — Why They Exist</h2>
    <p>Clothing brands and apparel buyers spend enormous amounts of time on calculations, research, and document preparation that should take minutes. An EU compliance check involves reading across five separate regulations. A landed cost calculation requires a FOB quote, a duty rate lookup, a freight estimate, and a margin calculation — across four different sources. An AQL inspection plan requires cross-referencing two ISO tables.</p>
    <p>SDF Clothing built these tools because our buyers needed them. Every tool on this page is used by brands and sourcing teams before they place an order. They are free because better-prepared buyers lead to better production outcomes for everyone.</p>

    <h2>AQL Quality Inspection — What the Standard Actually Means</h2>
    <p>AQL (Acceptable Quality Level) is the maximum percentage of defective units that can be accepted in a production lot. The garment industry standard is <strong style="color:#fff;">AQL 2.5</strong> — meaning up to 2.5% of inspected pieces can have major defects before the shipment is rejected.</p>
    <p>The AQL calculator determines two things: how many garments to inspect from a lot (sample size), and how many defects are allowable before rejection (acceptance number). Both depend on your lot size and inspection level. ISO 2859-1 General Inspection Level II is the default for most garment inspections.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Critical defects (safety risk) — AQL 0 or 0.065. Zero tolerance</li>
      <li>Major defects (functional or appearance) — AQL 2.5. Standard garment inspection</li>
      <li>Minor defects (cosmetic only) — AQL 4.0. Relaxed standard for minor issues</li>
    </ul>
    <p>SDF Clothing applies AQL 2.5 on every order before packing. Third-party inspection by SGS, Bureau Veritas, or Intertek is available on request.</p>

    <h2>GSM — The Most Important Number in Your Tech Pack</h2>
    <p>GSM (Grams per Square Metre) is the standard measure of fabric weight used across Bangladesh, Europe, and Asia. US suppliers often quote in oz/yd² instead. Getting this wrong — specifying 180 GSM when you want 180 oz — creates a catastrophic error in your sample fabric weight.</p>
    <p>The conversion is: <strong style="color:#fff;">GSM ÷ 33.906 = oz/yd²</strong>. Common reference points:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Lightweight T-shirt — 140–160 GSM (4.1–4.7 oz)</li>
      <li>Standard retail T-shirt — 170–200 GSM (5–5.9 oz)</li>
      <li>Heavyweight streetwear T-shirt — 220–260 GSM (6.5–7.7 oz)</li>
      <li>Standard hoodie (French terry) — 300–340 GSM (8.8–10 oz)</li>
      <li>Heavyweight hoodie (brushed fleece) — 380–420 GSM (11.2–12.4 oz)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Always specify GSM in your tech pack. Factories cannot price or produce accurately without it.</p>

    <h2>Fabric Shrinkage — Why It Matters Before You Cut</h2>
    <p>Fabric shrinks. Cotton shrinks 2–5% after first wash. If you cut 100 cm panels without accounting for 4% shrinkage, your finished garment will measure 96 cm — outside spec. For a 300-piece order of hoodies, that is 300 garments that do not fit as intended.</p>
    <p>Manufacturers test fabric shrinkage before cutting by washing a test swatch and measuring the dimensional change. The formula is: <strong style="color:#fff;">Shrinkage % = [(Pre-wash – Post-wash) / Pre-wash] × 100</strong>. Test both length (warp) and width (weft) — they shrink at different rates. SDF Clothing pre-shrinks all cotton fabrics before cutting for orders where dimensional stability is critical.</p>

    <h2>HS Codes for Clothing — What You Need to Know</h2>
    <p>Every garment imported internationally must be classified under a Harmonised System (HS) code. The correct code determines:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your import duty rate — a small difference in code can change duty from 12% to 0%</li>
      <li>Whether you can claim preferential duty-free access for Bangladesh-origin goods</li>
      <li>Whether quota or trade restriction applies</li>
      <li>Statistics and compliance documentation requirements</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Garment HS codes are structured around Chapter 61 (knitted and crocheted garments) and Chapter 62 (woven garments). T-shirts are 6109. Hoodies and sweatshirts are 6110. Men's woven trousers are 6203. Women's woven trousers are 6204. Always verify with a licensed customs broker for your specific destination market.</p>

    <h2>Care Label Requirements — EU, US, and Australia</h2>
    <p>Care labels are a legal requirement in the EU, US, and most major markets. Getting them wrong leads to product recalls, customs holds, and retailer chargebacks.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong style="color:#fff;">EU</strong> — ISO 3758 / GINETEX symbols required in the language of the country of sale. Five symbols in sequence: washing, bleaching, drying, ironing, professional care</li>
      <li><strong style="color:#fff;">USA</strong> — FTC Care Labeling Rule requires written care instructions in English. Symbols alone are not sufficient without text</li>
      <li><strong style="color:#fff;">Australia</strong> — ISO 3758 symbols accepted. Must be permanently attached and legible after care cycles</li>
    </ul>
    <p>SDF Clothing produces care labels in any language required for your destination market as part of standard production.</p>

